129
00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:28.520
So this is my dream as a product manager.

130
00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:31.320
This is the overview of the architecture

131
00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:35.080
that I started to create together with my CTO.

132
00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:38.520
I'm going to create an agent that is called Spec Reviewer.

133
00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:41.880
It's going to have access to specs.

134
00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:44.320
Specs will be saved in a ticketing system.

135
00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:48.080
It can be JIRA, it can be linear, it can be Monday notion,

136
00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:49.760
it can be on GitHub.

137
00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:52.000
It will be able to access designs.

138
00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:57.760
That is something that is not so standard to visual assets in the designs,

139
00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:01.800
and it will be able to access the feature that is deployed in

140
00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:05.600
staging environment or in preview environment and verify it.

141
00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:10.160
The way I see it is that agent is able to extract the requirements, all of them,

142
00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:13.400
and go every through every requirement

143
00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:17.440
that are in the specs and validate if they were really implemented or not.

144
00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:19.240
I'm thinking about an agent

145
00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:22.360
that is navigating through different types, impersonating

146
00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:26.240
to different roles into the product, and just going and clicking

147
00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:30.240
and trying different case cases in the product.

148
00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:35.160
So as a product manager, I'm already coding, I have access to the best tools.

149
00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:37.280
And I'm going to ask Claude,

150
00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:40.920
what does he think about this idea and if he can implement it.

151
00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:44.360
And the answer that I get is this.

152
00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:48.160
And this is the moment that I'm very concerned about, right?

153
00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:51.680
Like when my wife tells me this, I'm happy.

154
00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:55.920
But when Claude tells me this, I'm that's the point where I need to

155
00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:57.000
to be concerned.

156
00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:59.720
And I click enter and I get this.

157
00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:01.760
The reason I'm

158
00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:04.760
getting this is because it's a very hard task to do.

159
00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:10.720
And you can see here this error shows that the context window just exploded.

160
00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:15.320
So what I'm going to show now is how we took this simplistic idea

161
00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:19.800
of implementing an agent that is able to extract requirements

162
00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:22.800
and verifying them to make it actually work.

163
00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:26.840
So this was the first problem.

164
00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:30.080
The problem is that one agent just can't do it.

165
00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:34.280
One agent can take all the requirements that are in the ticket,

166
00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:36.200
all the requirements that are in the specs,

167
00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:38.600
all the design aspects that were introduced in

168
00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:39.960
this are the specific feature.

169
00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:42.080
And also go and verify them.

170
00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:44.000
It just explodes the agent.

171
00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:44.960
And by the way,

172
00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:48.960
that's probably one of the reasons that your coding agents don't do it.

173
00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:50.840
So what was

174
00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:53.840
our solution to deal with the crashing sessions?

175
00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:59.480
The idea was to to divide the task between two different agents.

176
00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:05.560
It's also it's another best practice you can use for other agent tasks.

177
00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:07.400
It's dividing it between planning.

178
00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:10.040
So we're going to have one agent that is the planner.

179
00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:13.600
The planners role is to extract the requirements

180
00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:17.840
from the spec and understand what are going to be the failure cases

181
00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:22.120
that I'm going to verify through the verification process.

182
00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:25.520
Only one task to extract requirements.

183
00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:28.240
The second agent is going to be the verification agent.

184
00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:32.120
The verification agent is going to navigate through different files,

185
00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:36.960
through the UI, through the design, and understand if the specific specs

186
00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:41.960
that were provided by the planner were were met in this feature.

187
00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:47.000
Okay, good news sessions are running.

188
00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:48.320
We're not crashing anymore,

189
00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:52.000
but unfortunately, the results that we're getting are horrible.

190
00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:58.080
What we understood is that the agents started

191
00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:01.040
skipping some of the requirements.

192
00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:05.000
We had tickets that included 10 or 12 requirements.

193
00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:09.160
Sometimes we had the process that included 20 requirements

194
00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:14.000
and the agent just consistently skipped some of them.

195
00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:15.880
The second thing that happened

196
00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:19.640
was that we noticed that the quality of the verification

197
00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:24.400
and the quality of the requirements that were extracted was inconsistent.

198
00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:28.240
And we understood that even though we divided the task between

199
00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:31.360
two different agents, we understood that it's

200
00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:34.360
still too much context for one agent.

201
00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:37.680
So this is going to be the only graph that I'm going

202
00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:40.880
to show in this presentation, and I promise.

203
00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:45.920
This is an illustration of the context problem that we're having in this task.

204
00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:50.880
So how the verification agent works, it gets the list from the planner

205
00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:54.480
of what is going to be the potential failure scenarios

206
00:11:54.480 --> 00:12:00.360
that I'm going to review, and starts with the first requirement from the spec

207
00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:05.560
going into specific files, navigating through them, testing the UI, checking

208
00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:11.200
if the requirement that is supposed to check was implemented or not, defines

209
00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:13.560
what would be the verdict, and proceeds to the next one

210
00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:14.960
and the next one, and the next one.

211
00:12:14.960 --> 00:12:18.400
You can understand that at this point, when I'm getting to the fifth or the sixth

212
00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:21.920
requirement, I'm having loads of contexts that are irrelevant

213
00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:26.840
for that specific task, and that makes the agent just dumber.

214
00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:29.440
The way to solve it

215
00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:33.360
would be to delegate the task between multiple subvariants.

216
00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:37.200
It's understanding that I can verify each of the sub

217
00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:40.680
requirements in the spec by different agents.

218
00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:46.320
So instead of one agent that is checking ten or 12 or 15 requirements

219
00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:51.520
sequentially, I'm going to have 15 or 12 agents that are running in parallel.

220
00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:54.000
Each of them is reaching to a specific verdict.

221
00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:57.760
And in the end, there's an orchestrator that collects all the verdicts

222
00:12:57.760 --> 00:13:00.760
at the end of the process.

223
00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:02.040
Great.

224
00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:03.760
We're having sessions that are running.

225
00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:06.920
We have all the requirements that are extracted and validated.

226
00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:10.320
Now we started to notice another problem that is related

227
00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:13.360
to the quality of the context.

228
00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:18.000
We notice that the agent is starting to make up

229
00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:21.320
requirements that nobody asked him to do.

230
00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:27.200
This is like an example from one of the first reports of our spec reviewer.

231
00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:28.520
You can see it on GitHub here.

232
00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:32.480
For example, you can see here the agent decided that we need to introduce

233
00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:36.160
a new responder command like nobody asked that.

234
00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:40.080
The second example is maintaining backward compatibility.

235
00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:44.000
This is again like something that the product manager,

236
00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:45.880
the CTO that designed the specific

237
00:13:45.880 --> 00:13:49.760
task, like nobody really had that specific requirement.

238
00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:54.640
The reason we understood what's happening here is the fact that we gave the agent

239
00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:56.840
only the specs.

240
00:13:56.840 --> 00:14:01.720
And what's really interesting here, and I added this git from inception,

241
00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:05.480
is the fact that when we gave the the agent the tickets,

242
00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:07.800
it only understood the specs and the design.

243
00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:11.960
It only understood what's going to be the goal.

244
00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:15.640
Like what is the intent in that specific moment.

245
00:14:15.640 --> 00:14:20.640
But without to be grounded with the code, it's just not connected to reality.

246
00:14:21.080 --> 00:14:25.520
The specs are a snapshot and what we're trying to achieve

247
00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:29.240
in this coding task, but the code is

248
00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:32.240
how we ground that agent to reality.

